messages:
mila: Heads up — hitting flaky SSE streams in the Node SDK when auth tokens rotate. We get JSON.parse errors because chunks split objects mid-field. Also saw a 401 that closed the stream mid-message. Anyone run into this?

ryan: Yup. Repro on my local when token refresh fires ~30s after connect. Fetch doesn't re-open a streaming body so we just get a truncated buffer and parse errors.

sarah: Is the stream NDJSON or SSE `data:` frames? That affects parsing strategy.

mila: It's standard SSE (server sends `event:`/`data:` blocks). The server sometimes sends large objects across multiple `data:` lines and client treats each `data:` as a separate chunk.

alex: Two quick things: 1) incremental JSON parsing (buffer until you can parse a full object) is the pragmatic client-side fix; 2) auth: make token lifetime > expected stream length or provide a refresh mechanism that doesn't interrupt SSE (hard).

ryan: Example pattern we've been using (pseudo-Node):
```js
let buffer = '';
for await (const chunk of fetchStream(response.body)) {
  buffer += chunk.toString();
  try {
    const obj = JSON.parse(buffer);
    handle(obj);
    buffer = '';
  } catch (e) {
    // not full JSON yet, keep buffering
  }
}
```
Problem: if server sends `{}{}{` (multiple objects concatenated) this simple approach misses boundaries.

sarah: Right — NDJSON (one JSON per line) or an explicit delimiter is much nicer. If server can switch to NDJSON or emit a `\n` after each object, parsing is trivial. Can the backend change?

mila: Backend can add a top-level delimiter but we can't always rely on it (3rd party models / proxies). Need robust client behavior too.

alex: For SSE specifically, you should respect `data:` lines: SSE allows multiline `data:` which should be concatenated with `\n` between lines, and each blank line delimits an event. So parsing at the event boundary is more correct than parsing arbitrary body chunks. Eg: accumulate per-event, then parse event.data as JSON.

ryan: Good call. Implementation sketch: accumulate bytes into a small state machine, split into events on `\n\n`, parse each event by joining its `data:` lines. That avoids mid-event partial JSON errors.

mila: That helps with split frames, but what about 401 mid-event? We lost the remainder. Any recommendations for resumability/backpressure?

alex: For resumability you need server support — include an event sequence id or cursor in each event. On reconnect you request `?since_id=XXX` or `Range`-style param. Otherwise client must treat reconnect as start-over and backend dedup/patch to be idempotent.

sarah: For backpressure: Node streams will keep delivering chunks; if consumer is slow, set a smaller highWaterMark on the Transform/readable or use async iteration and `await` the CPU-heavy work (so the loop naturally pauses). Piping to synchronous CPU work will blow memory.

ryan: Also watch gzip/compression. If server flushes compressed chunks, you might get mid-codepoint slices — use a streaming decoder (zlib.createGunzip() as stream) before the SSE parser.

mila: Good notes. Actionable checklist: 1) make Node client parse SSE event boundaries (not raw chunk boundaries); 2) add example that handles multiline `data:` and buffers per-event; 3) detect 401 and reconnect with exponential backoff + jitter; 4) recommend server emit event IDs or NDJSON for resumability. I'll open a short PR doc and a unit test.

alex: For 401 handling: when you get 401 mid-stream, emit a `stream_error` event and close, then try reconnect with backoff. If server supports short lived tokens that can be refreshed via a separate endpoint, do the refresh and then reconnect. Avoid trying to continue parsing an already-closed body.

ci-bot: Deployed static doc: "devex/sse-client-guidance.md" (preview link) :white_check_mark:

mila: Thanks — @ryan @alex can you review my PR when I open it? Will include a minimal SSE parser (stateful, respects `data:` concat) + test for split-data and 401.

ryan: On it. Quick suggestion for tests: simulate chunk boundaries that split UTF-8 multi-byte characters and JSON tokens. Helps catch decode bugs.

alex: I'll add a short code sample for backpressure (highWaterMark + async for-await). Also add a note about not holding large in-memory buffers for long-lived streams.

mila: Perfect. If folks agree I'll close this thread after PR lands. :thumbsup:
